WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.029
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:09.929
Due to popular request, today we will talk
about Neuralink, Elon Musk’s neural engineering

00:00:09.929 --> 00:00:14.289
company that he created to develop brain-machine
interfaces.

00:00:14.289 --> 00:00:18.430
And your first question likely is, why talk
about Neuralink now?

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:23.810
There was a recent event, and another one
last year as well, why did I not cover that?

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:27.650
Well, the launch event from last year indeed
promised a great deal.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:33.200
In this series, we often look at research
works that are just one year apart, and marvel

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.590
at the difference scientists have been able
to make in that tiny-tiny timeframe.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:44.560
So first, let’s talk about their paper from
2019, which will be incredible, and then,

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:50.470
see how far they have come in a year, which,
as you will see, is even more incredible.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:55.960
The promise is to be able to read and write
information to and from the brain.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:01.620
To accomplish this, as of 2019, they used
this robot to insert the electrodes into your

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:02.860
brain tissue.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.199
You can see the insertion process here.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:10.120
From the close-up image you might think that
this is a huge needle, but in fact, this needle

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.430
is extremely tiny, you can see a penny for
scale here.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:18.750
This is the story of how this rat got equipped
with a USB port.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:24.180
As this process is almost like inserting microphones
into its brain, now, we are able to read the

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.110
neural signals of this rat.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:31.530
Normally, these are analog signals, which
are read and digitized by Neuralink’s implant,

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:35.630
and now this brain data is represented as
a digital signal.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:39.229
Well, at first, this looks a bit like gibberish.

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:43.930
Do we really have to undergo a brain surgery
to get a bunch of these squiggly curves?

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:45.170
What do these really do for us?

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:49.049
Well, now that they are digitized, we can
have the Neuralink chip analyze these signals

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:51.720
and look for action potentials in them.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.409
These are also referred to as “spikes”
because of their shape.

00:01:55.409 --> 00:01:59.320
That sounds a bit better, but still, what
does this do for us?

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:00.740
Let’s see.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:03.670
Here we have a person with a mouse in their
hand.

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:08.110
This is an outward movement with the mouse,
and then, reaching back.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.209
Simple enough.

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:14.290
Now, what you see here below is the activity
of an example neuron.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:20.750
When nothing is happening, there is some firing,
but not much activity, and now, look!

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:26.310
When reaching out, this neuron fires a great
deal, and suddenly, when reaching back, again,

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.260
nearly no activity.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:33.510
This means that this neuron is tuned for an
outward motion, and this other one is tuned

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.430
for the returning motion.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:40.150
And all this is now information that we can
read in real time, and the more neurons we

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:43.260
can read, the more complex motion we can read.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.070
Absolutely incredible.

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:50.040
However, this is still a little difficult
to read, so let’s order them by what kind

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.620
of motion makes them excited.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:53.980
And there we go!

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:59.410
Suddenly, this is a much more organized way
to present all this neural activity, and now

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:03.480
we can detect what kind of motion the brain
is thinking about.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.060
This was the reading part, and that’s just
the start.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:12.300
What is even cooler is that we can invert
this process, read this spiking activity,

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:17.590
and just by looking at these, we can reconstruct
the motion the human wishes to perform.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:23.030
With this, brain-machine interfaces can be
created for people with all kinds of disabilities

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:27.160
where the brain can still think about the
movements, but the connection to the rest

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.069
of the body is severed.

00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:34.629
Now, these people only have to think about
moving, and then, the Neuralink device will

00:03:34.629 --> 00:03:37.830
read it and perform the cursor movement for
them.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:41.670
It really feels like we live in a science
fiction world.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:47.379
And all this signal processing is now possible
automatically and in real time, and all we

00:03:47.379 --> 00:03:53.160
need for this is this tiny-tiny chip that
takes just a few square millimeters.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:57.560
And don’t forget, that is just version one
from 2019.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:02.909
Now, onwards to the 2020 event, where, it
gets even better.

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:07.520
The Neuralink device has been placed into
Gertrude, the pig’s brain, and here, you

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:09.000
see it in action.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:14.019
We see the raster view here, and luckily,
we already know what it means, this lays bare

00:04:14.019 --> 00:04:19.969
the neural action potentials before our eyes,
or in other words, which neuron is spiking

00:04:19.969 --> 00:04:21.489
and exactly when.

00:04:21.489 --> 00:04:27.219
Below with blue, you see these activities
summed up for our convenience, and this way,

00:04:27.219 --> 00:04:33.159
you will not only see, but hear it too, as
these neurons are tuned for snout boops.

00:04:33.159 --> 00:04:38.180
In other words, you will see and hear that
the more the snout is stimulated, the more

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:44.449
neural activity it will show.

00:04:44.449 --> 00:04:49.610
Let’s listen.

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:53.430
And all this is possible today, and in real
time.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:59.419
That was one of the highlights of the 2020
progress update event, but it went further.

00:04:59.419 --> 00:05:00.529
Much further!

00:05:00.529 --> 00:05:01.529
Look!

00:05:01.529 --> 00:05:05.729
This is a pig on a treadmill, and here you
see the brain signal readings.

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:11.469
This signal marked with the circle shows where
a joint or limb is about to move, where the

00:05:11.469 --> 00:05:17.110
other, dimmer colored signal is the chip’s
prediction as to what is about to happen.

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:23.069
It takes into consideration periodicity, and
predicts higher-frequency movement, like these

00:05:23.069 --> 00:05:25.490
sharp turns really well.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:32.499
The two are almost identical, and that means
exactly what you think it means - today, we

00:05:32.499 --> 00:05:38.639
can not only read and write, but even predict
what the pig’s brain is about to do.

00:05:38.639 --> 00:05:43.029
And that was the part where I fell off the
chair when I watched this event live.

00:05:43.029 --> 00:05:50.460
You can also see the real and predicted world-space
positions for these body parts as well.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:51.979
Very close.

00:05:51.979 --> 00:05:56.749
Now note that there is a vast body of research
in brain-machine interfaces, and many of these

00:05:56.749 --> 00:06:02.479
things were possible in lab conditions, and
Neuralink’s quest here is to make them accessible

00:06:02.479 --> 00:06:04.810
for a wider audience within the next decade.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:10.080
If this project further improves at this rate,
it could help many paralyzed people around

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.439
the world live a longer, and more meaningful
life, and the neural enhancement aspect is

00:06:15.439 --> 00:06:17.919
also not out of question.

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:22.699
Just thinking about summoning your Tesla might
also summon it, which sounds like science

00:06:22.699 --> 00:06:28.199
fiction, and based on these results, you see
that it may even be one of the simplest tasks

00:06:28.199 --> 00:06:30.610
for a Neuralink chip in the future.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:35.960
And who knows, one day, maybe, with this device,
these videos could be beamed into your brain

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:41.699
much quicker, and this series would have to
be renamed from Two Minute Papers to Two Second

00:06:41.699 --> 00:06:45.889
Papers, or maybe even Two Microsecond Papers.

00:06:45.889 --> 00:06:50.449
They might actually fit into two minutes like
the title says, now that would truly be a

00:06:50.449 --> 00:06:51.639
miracle.

00:06:51.639 --> 00:06:55.800
Huge thanks to scientists at Neuralink for
our discussions about the concepts descriped

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.610
in this video and ensuring that you get accurate
information.

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:04.679
This is one of the reasons why our coverage
of the 2020 event is way too late compared

00:07:04.679 --> 00:07:09.979
to many mainstream media outlets, which leads
to a great deal less views for us, but it

00:07:09.979 --> 00:07:10.979
doesn’t matter.

00:07:10.979 --> 00:07:15.280
We are not maximizing views here, we are maximizing
learning.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:19.589
Note that they are also hiring, if you wish
to be a part of their vision and work with

00:07:19.589 --> 00:07:21.330
them, make sure to apply!

00:07:21.330 --> 00:08:07.289
The link is available

00:08:07.289 --> 00:08:17.909
in the video description.

00:08:17.909 --> 00:08:21.050
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

